    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Persepolis was raised on a great terrace by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> from about 519 to 515 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> and later kings added to it until the downfall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>.</li><li>It was a ceremonial complex used seasonally rather than one of the largest cities of the empire, and its function, including where the king's private quarters stood, is still not entirely clear.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> took it in 330 BCE, and soon afterwards its wooden parts were destroyed by fire; its ruins were visited by European travellers from the 15th century and excavated from 1930.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The study of the trilingual royal inscriptions found on the ruins of Persepolis led to the initial decipherment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> in the early 19th century.</p></aside>
    </section>

